as ambushed and killed by  gunmen on the outskirts of tehran on  friday  western intelligence agencies believe  fakarazadeh was behind a covert  iranian nuclear weapons programme  nobody's claimed responsibility for the  attack  but iran is blaming israel still ahead  sweden ramping up its relaxed pandemic  strategy  as a second wave is hitting the country  hard  sweden has so far resisted a national  lockdown but it's rethinking its  strategy after recording a surge of  second wave infections  tonight its citizens are being told to  stay home  strict measures also going into effect  as crystal gaban singh reports sweden  has been slammed for taking a lack's  attitude towards covet 19  but is now being hit with more deaths  than its neighbors  there are few masked faces in stockholm  but these  outdoor spaces are covered with those  familiar warnings  to keep your distance a second wave of  covet 19  is hitting sweden the big debates on the  tv all the time how we should  manage this and so on so i i don't think  we are easy going  we are we are affected by this disease  as every other country  dr johann lunegaard is the chief medical  officer at sweden's oldest university  hospital  he doesn't subscribe to the theory of  countries being coveted 19 winners or  losers  strategies work until they don't and  right now he says his staff is exhausted  and in a tough situation up to one-fifth  of all the patients in the hospital's  hospital or kobe patients  and almost half of the intensive care  unit patients  are covered patients at the moment  transmission rates in sweden are  substantially higher than a neighboring  norway denmark and finland with more  than 240  000 confirmed cases the loss of life in  other scandinavian countries where  lockdowns have been in place off and on  for months  is substantially less in sweden there's  been roughly 65 deaths per 100 000  people  while in denmark that number is roughly  14 in finland  it drops to 7 per 100 000 and in norway  it's six swedish officials admit there  appears to be little evidence of herd  immunity  and infections continue to rise in an  effort to curb social gatherings  nationally  alcohol cannot be sold after 10 pm in  restaurants bars or nightclubs  and formal public gatherings have been  reduced from 50 people  to eight what we do know from  local reports is that it has changed  behavior with people  we can see that more people are starting  to work from home under huston  sweden's prime minister stefan lowen  made a rare national address last  weekend telling people to cancel their  plans  and stay home but that is just a  suggestion  it seems like the the swedish population  take it more seriously  as infections and hospitalizations rise  the swedish statistical agency says that  for the first time  in a century life expectancy in the  country  will drop because of covid19  as for the current rate of transmission  it's hoped the new measures will start  to drive down infections  crystal gomansing global news london  the vatican is marking a historic moment  wilton gregory the archbishop  of washington dc has become the first  black cardinal  from the u.s the 72 year old was one of  13  new cardinals blessed by pope francis on  saturday  in june gregory blasted president  trump's visit to a historic washington  church  when soldiers had to use tear gas and  rubber bullets they say to clear  anti-racism protesters along the way  shop so they don't drop coming up how  local businesses are  banking on your loyalty  well despite pleas from health officials  across the country  thousands of canadians spent black  friday in a shopping centre  this is what it looked like yesterday in  calgary's largest mall  many shoppers were wearing masks but  there was almost no physical distancing  police had to be called in to disperse  the crowd in ontario at least six  businesses in the york region were fined  for not following public health rules  well this weekend  is typically one of the busiest shopping  times of the year but  many stores have been forced to close or  stop in-person sales because of the  pandemic  this time of year also crucial for the  survival of small businesses  and as catherine ward explains there's  concern that online sales alone  may not be enough to save them  there is just something just to be said  for the  human connection kara kennedy has run  her high-end consignment shop in toronto  for the last four and a half years  people have always been able to shop  online from anywhere in the world  but she says it's the in-store purchases  that have been key  for the bottom line there's a lot of  stuff in our store that's pretty high  quality  and you really have to touch and feel it  to to want to buy it in person  so it's really hard to replicate that  experience online last year 75 percent  of kennedy's sales came from in-store  purchases with more lockdowns rolling  out  small businesses across the country  continue to struggle  as governments try to balance  restrictions and safety  in light of covid19 recent data from the  canadian federation of independent  business suggests  only 38 percent of retail shops are  hitting normal sales compared  to this time last year and one in seven  are at risk of shutting down permanently  on monday the canadian government is  expected to deliver its fall  economic statement experts say they hope  more will be offered for small and  medium-sized stores there are still tens  of thousands of businesses slipping  through the cracks of the wage subsidy  the rent subsidy and the sibo loan  program  we're hoping that the government will  announce fixes to them especially for  new businesses new businesses started in  2020  they don't qualify for any of the  programs digital experts say  investing in paid advertising can help  leverage  a small business online but they say the  best thing owners can do  is to tap into their current customer  base what are you doing for your loyal  customers are you giving them incentives  to sort of share what are you doing with  the email list that you already have are  you  you know emailing out customers telling  them about promotions telling what you  have  kennedy says so far she has seen an  increase in online sales  and remains thankful for the customers  who are choosing  local options they know that small  business is important and people are  actively seeking to buy from small  business  and i think that is going to save us  catherine ward global news toronto  and that is global national for this  saturday i'm sonia diol tonight's your  canada is the snowy fields outside of  airdrie  in alberta do send your photos to  viewers at globalnational.com thanks  very much for watching  i'll see you back here tomorrow good  night
 

